window.docContents[11].resolve({"/Introduction/#pde-introduction":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nImagine a rod with a warm middle and both ends held at zero temperature.\nHeat flows toward the cold ends, and the temperature falls. The heat equation\ndescribes that evolution. An explicit formula gives one solution; an energy\nestimate proves uniqueness and controls changes in the initial temperature.\n\nOn a rod with its ends held at zero temperature, a sine-shaped profile decays\nwithout changing shape:\n\nHere time and length have been scaled so that the heat equation is\n. Differentiating once in time and twice in space gives\n\nSo : the profile satisfies the heat equation. It also vanishes at\nboth ends of the rod, and at time zero it is . These are separate\nfacts that together identify the solution and its data.\n\nThe first calculation checks .\n\n\n\nThere is a reason this calculation closes so neatly. The second derivative\nof  is , so substituting  reduces the\nPDE to . A spatial eigenfunction has turned the evolution into\nan ordinary differential equation. For the higher mode , with\npositive integer , the same calculation gives\n. Finer oscillations decay faster.\n\nThe same initial shape behaves differently on a vibrating string. With zero\ninitial velocity, the wave equation gives .\nBoth ends stay fixed in each problem. Compare their evolution below.\n\n\n\nFor the heat mode, try . Its amplitude at time  equals the amplitude\nof the first mode at time . At the same amplitude, the extra oscillations\nmake the spatial gradient larger and increase the rate of heat dissipation. For the wave\nmode, displacement and velocity exchange energy as the string vibrates.\n\nThe calculation also exposes what has not yet been answered. It gives one\nsolution; uniqueness needs an argument that applies to every solution in a\nspecified class. Replacing the initial sine by a rough temperature profile\nrequires a construction that survives limits. Replacing the rod by all of\nspace leads to the Gaussian kernel, whose initial point mass is understood\nthrough convergence against test functions rather than pointwise substitution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"1. Introduction","id":"/Introduction/#pde-introduction"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation--Cole___Hopf___-a-nonlinear-to-linear-transformation--Exact-profiles":{"contents":"The simplest heat profile to which the transform applies is ,\nfor which  and the quotient collapses to the constant\n at unit viscosity. The quotient of  is the same constant,\nalthough  does not solve the heat equation; in the forward direction\nthere is no such ambiguity, since the transform is a function of .\n\nFor the heat solution  and unit viscosity, the Cole–Hopf\ntransform is the constant velocity , which solves viscous Burgers on\nall spacetime. Positivity of the exponential discharges the denominator\ncondition, while symbolic differentiation verifies the closed-form\ntransform identity.\n\nFor , both  and  equal , so  solves the\nunit heat equation and is everywhere positive. Also , hence the\nCole–Hopf quotient is . All derivatives of this constant vanish,\nso its Burgers residual is zero.\n\nA constant velocity does not exercise the nonlinear term. Adding the\nreflected exponential  keeps  a positive heat solution, since\neach summand solves the heat equation separately, but makes \n-dependent: the velocity is , and it is stationary because\nthe common factor  cancels in the quotient.\n\nThe positive heat profilehas zero heat residual, and its ordinary Cole–Hopf transform agrees\npointwise withThe algebraically equivalent stationary velocity\n has zero unit-viscosity Burgers residual everywhere.\nPositivity of the exponential denominators discharges the quotient side\nconditions. In this stationary example, nonlinear advection and viscosity\nbalance each other while the time derivative vanishes.\n\nDifferentiate the two exponential summands separately; each solves the unit\nheat equation, so their sum does as well and is strictly positive. Substituting\n into  gives the displayed velocity. Multiply\nnumerator and denominator by  to obtain\n. Direct differentiation of this stationary expression,\nfollowed by clearing its positive denominator, reduces the Burgers residual\nto a polynomial identity that normalizes to zero.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Cole–Hopf Transformation\u0009Cole–Hopf: a nonlinear-to-linear transformation","header":"9.2.1.5.1.3. Exact profiles","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation--Cole___Hopf___-a-nonlinear-to-linear-transformation--Exact-profiles"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Trace___-flat___-curved___-Hilbert___-and-finite-exponent":{"contents":"Trace is never pointwise evaluation of an arbitrary  representative.\nThe construction starts on a smooth core, proves an estimate, and extends by\ndensity to the completed graph space.\n\nThe completed trace theory contains:\n\n* endpoint evaluation on the positive half-line;* the flat upper-half-space trace;* a chart-assembled trace on every operational  domain, independent of\nthe atlas and characterized by literal restriction on functions continuous\nup to the boundary;* the same trace for Banach-valued functions, commuting with bounded linear\nmaps of the target and reducing to the scalar trace for real values;* convergence of strip averages \nto the flat trace for , with an explicit rate;* a Hilbert trace quotient and its canonical minimal-energy extension;* identification of the half-space Hilbert trace quotient with real\n, with a bounded lifting;* on operational  domains, identification of the Hilbert trace quotient\nwith chartwise  boundary data, a bounded lifting, and\nequivalence of the chartwise spaces of two atlases;* a finite-exponent trace for every real ;* finite-exponent Banach trace quotients realizing the exact algebraic ranges;* for , the half-space trace into the Gagliardo space\n, a bounded mollified lifting, and the\nidentification of the trace quotient with that space, for scalar and\nBanach-valued functions;* canonical operator, kernel, and quotient-space compatibility at ;* agreement with literal restriction on the ambient smooth core;* the characterization of  as the trace kernel on the half-space\nand on  domains, for every finite , for scalar and\nBanach-valued functions;* higher-order zero-boundary closures ;* first-order slices of lower derivative coordinates into .\n\nIn the Hilbert case, quotienting by the trace kernel gives an abstract boundary\nHilbert space.  Orthogonal projection onto the kernel complement supplies a\ncanonical norm-preserving minimal extension. On the half-space this\nquotient is real . A Fourier estimate\nconstructs the trace and an explicit bounded lifting; agreement on smooth\nfunctions and density show that its physical  value is the existing\nboundary trace. The two maps identify the quotient and fractional norms up\nto proved bounds in both directions. On an operational  domain the\nsame quotient is identified with boundary data whose localized chart\ncoordinates lie in , again with a bounded lifting and\ntwo-sided norm bounds, and the chartwise spaces of two atlases are\nequivalent.\n\nThe flat Fourier construction also works at every real order :\nrestriction to a hyperplane extends to a continuous map\n with a bounded right inverse.\nThe half derivative is lost when we integrate out the normal frequency.\nA weighted Cauchy–Schwarz estimate controls this integral, and its weight\ngives an explicit lifting. Agreement with literal restriction on Schwartz\nfunctions connects the Fourier operator to boundary values.\n\nAt finite exponent, quotienting by the trace kernel gives a canonical Banach\ntrace space. Its boundary-value map is continuous and injective, its image is\nexactly the operator range, and the quotient is linearly equivalent to that\nalgebraic range. At , canonical exponent transport identifies this\noperator, its kernel, and its quotient trace space with the Hilbert\nconstruction, both on the half-space and on operational  domains.\nOn the half-space and for , the trace satisfies the\nGagliardo condition of order : splitting a boundary difference\nthrough points at height  and applying Hardy's inequality in the\nnormal direction bounds its fractional energy. Mollifying the boundary data\nat scale  and cutting off in  gives a bounded right inverse, so the\ntrace quotient is the Gagliardo space  with\nequivalent norms. Both arguments use only the norm of the target and hold\nfor Banach-valued functions. On operational  domains and for\n the range of the trace is characterized chartwise: a boundary\nfunction is a trace exactly when each localized chart coordinate lies in the\nGagliardo space of order , and a bounded lifting recovers it from\nthose coordinates.\n\nWeighted Hilbert graph spaces are defined by completing the weighted row\nnorm, which is complete for any nonnegative densities. Positivity of the\nzeroth weight, lower bounds, and two-sided bounds give successively stronger\nidentifications with function spaces, and upper bounds give smooth density\non . A weighted reflection bound and the Hölder step of\nweighted interpolation are proved.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.5. Trace: flat, curved, Hilbert, and finite exponent","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Trace___-flat___-curved___-Hilbert___-and-finite-exponent"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Fractional-spaces-on-a-domain":{"contents":"There is no single canonical definition of  on an arbitrary\nset for every real . The library therefore keeps three constructions\nseparate: restrictions of whole-space elements, the intrinsic\nGagliardo–Slobodeckij space, and the closure of supported Schwartz\nfunctions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.6. Fractional spaces on a domain","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Fractional-spaces-on-a-domain"}});